# Every file is checked out with exactly the bytes that were committed, on every system and
# whatever the clone's core.autocrlf says: no line ending is ever converted. The build and the
# tests pin the exact bytes of many source files by SHA-256 (the shader sources, their manifests
# and embedded SPIR-V, the media worker's build identity, signed fixtures, license and dependency
# records), and a Windows clone that turned LF into CRLF would fail those checks or build
# something else than the other systems. One rule for all files, so a file pinned later is
# covered too. The repository's hygiene check keeps this rule first and refuses any rule that
# turns conversion back on. The rules below are kept for what they say about why their bytes
# matter.
* -text

# Digest-bound dependency artifacts must never be line-ending normalized or otherwise rewritten:
# the intake contract binds their exact bytes to SHA-256 records.
dependencies/unicode/** -text
dependencies/licenses/** -text
# The CPython patch changes Visual Studio project files, which have Windows line endings; its
# context lines must keep them or it no longer applies.
dependencies/patches/python/** -text
assets/ocio/** -text

# Vendored interface assets are digest-bound in their provenance records (src/ui/kit/third_party/
# */provenance.md): their exact bytes are what those SHA-256 values cover, so they must never be
# line-ending normalized or otherwise rewritten.
src/ui/kit/third_party/** -text

# A project and document as an older build wrote them: the migration test compares their exact bytes.
tests/fixtures/paths/** -text

# The update tests' fixtures are signed: a signature covers their exact bytes.
src/update/tests/fixtures/** -text
src/update/tests/reference/*.zsync -text

# Licenses of the prebuilt update runtimes and installer tool are digest-bound in their records.
dependencies/update-runtime/licenses/** -text
dependencies/installer-tools/licenses/** -text
dependencies/appimage-runtime/licenses/** -text
